{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>If you lend money to My people.</b> Rabbi Yishmael says: every 'if' in the Torah is optional except for three, and this is one of them (Mekhilta)." "<b>To My people.</b> My people precedes a gentile; a poor person precedes a rich person; your poor precede the poor of your city; the poor of your city precede the poor of another city. And this is its meaning: 'If you lend money' — lend it to My people and not to a gentile; and to which of My people? To the poor person; and which poor person? To the one who is with you. Another interpretation: 'To My people' — do not treat him with contempt in the matter of the loan, for he is My people." "<b>With you.</b> Regard yourself as though you are the poor person." "<b>You shall not be to him as a creditor.</b> Do not press him forcefully. If you know that he does not have it, do not treat him as though you had lent to him, but as though you had not lent to him — that is, do not put him to shame." "<b>Interest.</b> Increase, for it is like the bite of a snake, which bites a small wound on the foot and he does not feel it, and suddenly it swells and blows up to his skull — so too increase is not felt and not noticed until the increase mounts and deprives him of much money (Tanchuma)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:59.523004931Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}